.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii proc.5
.\"
.TH PROC 5 "2026-03-12" "Substrate Kernel" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
proc \- process information pseudo-filesystem
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B proc
filesystem is a pseudo-filesystem which provides an interface to kernel
data structures. It is commonly mounted at
.IR /proc .
.PP
Most of the files in the proc filesystem are read-only, but some may be
writable for process control purposes.
.SS Files and Directories
The following files are available in the root of the proc filesystem:
.TP
.B /proc/cpuinfo
CPU information: processor type, vendor, MHz, cache size, and capabilities.
.TP
.B /proc/meminfo
Memory usage statistics: total, free, buffers, cached, swap.
.TP
.B /proc/memtrack
Physical-page accounting by allocating call site.
One line per site:
.BI site= address
(the caller's return address, to be resolved against the kernel's symbols),
.BI alloc= n
and
.BI free= n
(pages over the site's lifetime) and
.BI live= n
(pages still allocated).
Every page leaving and returning to the buddy allocator is counted, so a site
whose
.I live
count keeps climbing is leaking.
A final
.B total
line adds
.BI free_pages= n ,
the free page count read at the same moment: the sum of total
.I live
and
.I free_pages
stays constant while every allocation path is accounted.
.TP
.B /proc/uma
The slab allocator's zones, one per line (cf. Linux
.IR /proc/slabinfo ):
the zone's name,
.BI size= bytes
per object,
.B allocs=
and
.B frees=
over its lifetime,
.B cur=
objects in use,
.B cached=
objects freed but held in the per-CPU and depot caches, and the
.B slabs=
and
.B pages=
the zone holds.
.TP
.B /proc/uptime
System uptime in seconds and idle time.
.TP
.B /proc/cmdline
Kernel boot command line as passed by the bootloader.
.TP
.B /proc/version
Kernel version string including build date.
.TP
.B /proc/loadavg
System load averages for the last 1, 5, and 15 minutes, followed by the number of currently executing kernel scheduling entities over the total number of scheduling entities, and finally the PID of the most recently created process.
.TP
.B /proc/cow_stats
Copy-on-Write memory statistics: page faults, COW duplications, TLB flushes.
.TP
.B /proc/filesystems
List of supported filesystems.
.TP
.B /proc/ioports
Allocated I/O port resource tree exported by the kernel resource manager.
.TP
.B /proc/iomem
Allocated MMIO resource tree exported by the kernel resource manager.
.TP
.B /proc/pci
Enumerated PCI devices as seen by the kernel bus layer.
.TP
.B /proc/devtree
Registered kernel bus/device hierarchy dump.
.TP
.B /proc/device-events
Recent kernel device-model add, remove, bind, and unbind events.
.SS Per-Process Directories
For each running process with PID
.IR n ,
there exists a directory
.BI /proc/ n /
containing the following files:
.TP
.BI /proc/ n /status
Process status information: name, state, PID, UID, GID.
.TP
.BI /proc/ n /cmdline
Command line used to start the process.
.SH NOTES
The proc filesystem is implemented using a table-driven architecture,
making it easy to add new entries. Each entry is defined by a generator
function that produces the file contents on read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (2),
.BR procfs (7)
